1
00:00:06,773 --> 00:00:12,395
Martin Heidegger's seminal essay, The Question Concerning Technology, is an important one,

2
00:00:12,996 --> 00:00:18,958
not only for consideration of exactly what its title talks about, the notion of technology,

3
00:00:19,698 --> 00:00:24,160
what technology is, the effects that it has on us, how it interfaces with the human being,

4
00:00:24,841 --> 00:00:29,583
but it's also helpful, I think, for understanding Heidegger's attitudes toward

5
00:00:30,483 --> 00:00:35,465
not only modernity, but also towards metaphysics and towards being more generally.

6
00:00:36,096 --> 00:00:42,458
But it really is an essay that is focused critically on our

7
00:00:42,858 --> 00:00:49,659
current modern condition, whether we understand that as the modernity of Heidegger's own time,

8
00:00:49,939 --> 00:00:55,841
the middle of the last century, or if we understand it in terms of what people call late modernity

9
00:00:55,901 --> 00:00:58,722
or post-modernity, late capitalism.

10
00:00:59,402 --> 00:01:01,322
There's a lot of different names for it.

11
00:01:02,303 --> 00:01:08,185
The current condition that we're in, where these questions and these issues that Heidegger

12
00:01:08,225 --> 00:01:11,747
has his finger on are becoming even more pressing.

13
00:01:11,767 --> 00:01:13,708
They're sort of raised to a higher power.

14
00:01:14,429 --> 00:01:16,770
So this is a very important essay.

15
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:19,672
Fortunately, it's fairly short.

16
00:01:19,932 --> 00:01:20,092
It's

17
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:23,494
not as dense as all of Heidegger's works.

18
00:01:23,814 --> 00:01:30,338
It ends up making reference to quite a lot of other themes like truth or Aletheia

19
00:01:30,658 --> 00:01:33,300
as revealing or unconcealing.

20
00:01:34,096 --> 00:01:37,738
He talks about Aristotle's Four Causes in a very interesting way.

21
00:01:38,539 --> 00:01:43,822
He's focused on the question of essence that we'll get to in just a few moments.

22
00:01:45,343 --> 00:01:51,706
But before we do that, I wanted to sort of give you a general thumbnail sketch of where he's

23
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:53,087
going with all of this.

24
00:01:54,748 --> 00:02:00,651
Heidegger thinks that our conventional sort of knee-jerk,

25
00:02:01,072 --> 00:02:04,338
immediate, conceptions about technology

26
00:02:05,119 --> 00:02:07,361
are, well, in a certain sense, correct.

27
00:02:07,801 --> 00:02:09,922
They're also, in a certain sense, off-base.

28
00:02:09,962 --> 00:02:13,445
They conceal more than they reveal to us.

29
00:02:14,085 --> 00:02:20,110
They don't actually show us what technology really is and what technology really does

30
00:02:21,290 --> 00:02:23,332
and how the human being,

31
00:02:24,212 --> 00:02:25,613
as such, Dasein,

32
00:02:26,434 --> 00:02:28,275
is intimately connected with it.

33
00:02:28,315 --> 00:02:30,557
It tends to take a kind of external

34
00:02:31,443 --> 00:02:32,484
view on it that

35
00:02:33,284 --> 00:02:37,086
doesn't necessarily leave the human being out, but even doesn't fully understand

36
00:02:37,866 --> 00:02:40,228
how human beings interface with technology.

37
00:02:41,088 --> 00:02:42,289
And what he's going to do

38
00:02:43,049 --> 00:02:47,711
is lead us away from that sort of perspective very early on,

39
00:02:48,812 --> 00:02:52,534
what he calls the instrumental and anthropological perspective on technology.

40
00:02:54,155 --> 00:02:57,476
He's going to bring us into traditional metaphysics, not

41
00:02:58,177 --> 00:03:00,318
in order to endorse it wholesale,

42
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:02,540
But in order to say that

43
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:07,941
Aristotle was right about certain things, but also didn't adequately

44
00:03:08,821 --> 00:03:14,062
think through or conceptualize or carry out his work as a phenomenologist

45
00:03:14,822 --> 00:03:16,683
to the degree that he ought to have,

46
00:03:17,703 --> 00:03:22,484
then Heidegger is going to go on from there to draw a lot of conclusions having to do with

47
00:03:22,524 --> 00:03:26,384
this notion of truth and revealing and what techne,

48
00:03:27,424 --> 00:03:30,305
the word we get technology from, consists in.

49
00:03:31,177 --> 00:03:35,479
how it's tied in with episteme, science, disciplinary knowledge.

50
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:38,281
Then he's going to

51
00:03:39,241 --> 00:03:44,624
bring us to sort of a crux where he says, is modern technology,

52
00:03:45,585 --> 00:03:49,767
is the technology of the mechanized industrial age,

53
00:03:50,688 --> 00:03:54,810
is it something radically different from previous technology?

54
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:57,372
Because remember, human beings have had technology

55
00:03:58,152 --> 00:03:59,313
from the beginning of history,

56
00:04:00,336 --> 00:04:03,459
actually prehistory, you know, if you think about flint wrapping

57
00:04:04,379 --> 00:04:09,063
and skinning animals and producing pellets, that's early technology.

58
00:04:09,083 --> 00:04:10,264
That's doing something

59
00:04:11,205 --> 00:04:13,787
with the natural environment, making tools.

60
00:04:15,268 --> 00:04:18,631
Very early on, we figured out how to make tools to make other tools.

61
00:04:19,191 --> 00:04:22,114
Aristotle, by his own time, so we're talking about

62
00:04:23,975 --> 00:04:29,179
23, 24 centuries ago, already is talking about the hand as the tool of tools.

63
00:04:30,522 --> 00:04:36,446
and talking about slaves and soul tools and speculating, by the way, ourselves when we gave

64
00:04:36,466 --> 00:04:38,287
us the word technology, technologoi,

65
00:04:40,428 --> 00:04:47,353
speculating about the possibility of these kind of fairy tale things like looms that

66
00:04:47,373 --> 00:04:50,815
would weave themselves and how that would transform society.

67
00:04:52,325 --> 00:04:55,448
So the notion of technology has been with us for a long time.

68
00:04:55,748 --> 00:05:02,214
Is it radically different in the modern age, whether we're talking about the early modern age

69
00:05:02,794 --> 00:05:05,357
when natural science is becoming

70
00:05:06,357 --> 00:05:12,983
really physics and then chemistry and later on biology are becoming the basis for a lot of

71
00:05:13,063 --> 00:05:14,825
thinking and working things out?

72
00:05:16,582 --> 00:05:20,765
whether we're talking about the industrial age, where now we have tools being used to create

73
00:05:20,805 --> 00:05:25,908
tools, being used to create tools, and so on and so forth, or whether we're talking about

74
00:05:26,848 --> 00:05:29,730
what they nowadays call post-industrial society.

75
00:05:30,690 --> 00:05:31,851
Heidegger's asking,

76
00:05:33,192 --> 00:05:34,933
does all of that change the game?

77
00:05:35,173 --> 00:05:36,653
Is there a radical shift?

78
00:05:36,794 --> 00:05:38,154
And his answer is yes and no.

79
00:05:39,315 --> 00:05:43,117
He's going to bring in this conception of what he calls standing reserves.

80
00:05:44,412 --> 00:05:45,933
In German, Bestand.

81
00:05:46,834 --> 00:05:51,016
And this is going to be something that is,

82
00:05:52,117 --> 00:05:56,739
you could say, essential, although that's perhaps not exactly the right word for it.

83
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:01,402
It is the central feature in the modern attitude towards technology.

84
00:06:02,123 --> 00:06:03,403
So that's where we're going.

85
00:06:03,984 --> 00:06:06,805
You notice I've put a few things up here as starting points.

86
00:06:08,086 --> 00:06:11,348
He's questioning about the essence of technology.

87
00:06:12,323 --> 00:06:13,483
Early on he says,

88
00:06:16,024 --> 00:06:17,925
we'll be questioning concerning technology.

89
00:06:18,025 --> 00:06:19,426
Questioning builds away.

90
00:06:20,006 --> 00:06:26,668
So he's not concerned with simply asking a question and then like a catechist looking for answers

91
00:06:26,708 --> 00:06:30,390
right away. Questioning itself is a mode of thinking.

92
00:06:30,910 --> 00:06:33,251
And it's not something that you want to hasten.

93
00:06:33,771 --> 00:06:39,193
It's not something that you want to try to place within categories from the beginning.

94
00:06:39,769 --> 00:06:45,151
You want, true questioning means being attentive to what it is that you're asking questions

95
00:06:45,171 --> 00:06:47,931
about. If you're questioning a person, you actually want to pay attention to them.

96
00:06:48,331 --> 00:06:53,593
You don't want to pigeonhole them immediately and not allow them to express what it is that

97
00:06:53,613 --> 00:06:59,315
they have to express. If you're questioning concerning a text, you actually want to read the

98
00:06:59,375 --> 00:07:01,195
text, let the text work on you.

99
00:07:01,655 --> 00:07:07,057
If you're questioning, this is kind of a trivial instance, questioning concerning coffee, you

100
00:07:07,097 --> 00:07:07,237
know,

101
00:07:08,041 --> 00:07:09,402
What kind of coffee is this?

102
00:07:09,462 --> 00:07:10,642
Where did it come from?

103
00:07:11,422 --> 00:07:14,384
What does it taste like? What does the taste reveal to me?

104
00:07:14,724 --> 00:07:18,125
You have to be patient in order to question adequately.

105
00:07:20,286 --> 00:07:22,967
Questioning is part of Heidegger's phenomenological

106
00:07:23,808 --> 00:07:24,928
or metaphysical method.

107
00:07:26,229 --> 00:07:26,769
So he says,

108
00:07:27,990 --> 00:07:33,332
we want to question concerning technology and in so doing we would like to prepare a free relationship

109
00:07:33,372 --> 00:07:38,616
to it. So that's going to be a key concern running through this entire thing.

110
00:07:39,216 --> 00:07:43,898
A free relationship. And I put this in quotes because that's the term that he uses.

111
00:07:44,499 --> 00:07:46,240
What is a free relationship?

112
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:51,682
He tells us, and this is an early sort of sketch of it, the relationship will be free if it

113
00:07:51,922 --> 00:07:54,483
opens our human existence to the

114
00:07:55,284 --> 00:08:01,527
essence of technology. So already here, we're looking at a connection between existence and

115
00:08:01,787 --> 00:08:04,792
essence. We're trying to see how they fuse together.

116
00:08:05,732 --> 00:08:09,015
How does my existence as a human being,

117
00:08:10,136 --> 00:08:12,858
how does that find itself revealed

118
00:08:13,859 --> 00:08:13,899
in

119
00:08:14,679 --> 00:08:21,325
finding out what the essence, not the instances, not just the examples, not just how it affects

120
00:08:21,365 --> 00:08:21,525
me,

121
00:08:22,505 --> 00:08:24,467
but the essence of technology.

122
00:08:25,368 --> 00:08:28,290
Human beings, by the way, for Heidegger,

123
00:08:29,043 --> 00:08:32,285
are by their very nature questioning beings.

124
00:08:32,345 --> 00:08:36,707
That is part of what makes us the kind of being that we are, the very special kind of being

125
00:08:36,727 --> 00:08:39,448
that he calls Dasein. So

126
00:08:40,588 --> 00:08:44,911
where he's going to go from this is thinking about technology as a

127
00:08:46,291 --> 00:08:46,731
means,

128
00:08:48,132 --> 00:08:53,034
as a human activity. And he's going to say, this is actually quite correct.

129
00:08:53,334 --> 00:08:56,436
And then what he's going to do is say, it is correct.

130
00:08:58,883 --> 00:08:59,984
but it's superficial.

131
00:09:02,486 --> 00:09:07,449
When we're engaged in philosophy, if we're doing the kind of philosophy that Heidegger is,

132
00:09:08,690 --> 00:09:10,111
we may acknowledge

133
00:09:10,852 --> 00:09:12,233
the correctness of the correct.

134
00:09:12,633 --> 00:09:16,376
We may acknowledge its usefulness for communication.

135
00:09:17,737 --> 00:09:19,398
We don't have to throw it away.

136
00:09:19,598 --> 00:09:22,160
We don't have to reject it as some sort of

137
00:09:23,241 --> 00:09:25,843
neo-Luddites with respect to technology.

138
00:09:26,868 --> 00:09:32,530
But what we have to recognize is that everything that's genuinely important, when we go to

139
00:09:32,571 --> 00:09:35,012
its roots, to its foundation, to its essence,

140
00:09:37,613 --> 00:09:38,813
this is not going to help us.

141
00:09:40,494 --> 00:09:41,414
As a matter of fact,

142
00:09:42,855 --> 00:09:44,216
this can stand in the way.

143
00:09:45,076 --> 00:09:51,379
This ends up distracting us, providing us a screen, a concealment, to use Heidegger's terms,

144
00:09:52,039 --> 00:09:52,499
from

145
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:53,960
carrying out

146
00:09:59,103 --> 00:10:02,185
A genuinely metaphysical inquiry.

147
00:10:03,105 --> 00:10:09,008
And by metaphysical, he means revealing to us, gradually, stage by stage,

148
00:10:10,609 --> 00:10:15,772
aided, by the way, by the insights of previous metaphysicians like Plato or Aristotle or all

149
00:10:15,792 --> 00:10:16,673
the way to Nietzsche,

150
00:10:17,393 --> 00:10:20,475
showing to us what the essences of the things are.

151
00:10:21,695 --> 00:10:24,777
That's an activity that takes quite a bit of work.

152
00:10:25,262 --> 00:10:28,404
So let's get ourselves up to that point.

153
00:10:29,144 --> 00:10:31,766
He starts out by saying, technology

154
00:10:33,207 --> 00:10:35,408
is not equivalent to the essence of technology.

155
00:10:36,048 --> 00:10:38,370
What does he mean by that little phrase?

156
00:10:38,950 --> 00:10:42,472
Technology in the sense of what we're used to encountering.

157
00:10:43,753 --> 00:10:49,996
Technology can be as crude as writing on a chalkboard, or diagramming things on a chalkboard,

158
00:10:50,437 --> 00:10:50,957
or the press

159
00:10:53,218 --> 00:10:58,740
which took the chalk and pressed it into this form and then put it into boxes, which then were

160
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:00,681
sold, which then made their way to me.

161
00:11:01,261 --> 00:11:06,603
And I believe I probably took this out of a classroom, came in my pocket because I have a tendency

162
00:11:06,623 --> 00:11:06,723
to

163
00:11:07,443 --> 00:11:09,364
shove chalk in my pocket as I lecture.

164
00:11:09,384 --> 00:11:13,805
And, you know, if you think about it, there's technology involved in all sorts of steps there.

165
00:11:14,346 --> 00:11:17,467
There's all sorts of examples or instances of technology.

166
00:11:18,007 --> 00:11:19,447
These glasses are technology.

167
00:11:20,128 --> 00:11:21,288
They allow me to be able to see.

168
00:11:21,868 --> 00:11:24,329
We're using technology right now to record this.

169
00:11:26,130 --> 00:11:28,351
None of those are the essence of technology.

170
00:11:29,172 --> 00:11:30,372
So what does he mean by essence?

171
00:11:30,392 --> 00:11:30,832
He says,

172
00:11:31,633 --> 00:11:37,015
when we're seeking the essence of tree, for example, we have to become aware of what pervades

173
00:11:37,416 --> 00:11:38,276
every tree as

174
00:11:39,577 --> 00:11:39,937
tree,

175
00:11:40,657 --> 00:11:44,399
and that it's not itself a tree that can be encountered among the other trees.

176
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:49,043
The essence of technology will not be a piece of technology.

177
00:11:49,103 --> 00:11:53,026
It will not be one single pattern or mode of technology.

178
00:11:53,686 --> 00:11:57,969
Very often we go astray when we're trying to think of the essences of things by identifying

179
00:11:58,009 --> 00:12:04,514
them with one particular example or one paradigmatic example and saying that that one can then

180
00:12:04,574 --> 00:12:05,775
substitute for everything.

181
00:12:07,436 --> 00:12:08,897
That's not really the case.

182
00:12:09,898 --> 00:12:11,599
Not at least when we're doing metaphysics.

183
00:12:11,819 --> 00:12:12,760
We want to know

184
00:12:13,725 --> 00:12:15,867
for all the things that we call technology,

185
00:12:17,268 --> 00:12:18,889
what is it that they have in common?

186
00:12:19,930 --> 00:12:22,251
What runs through all of them

187
00:12:22,952 --> 00:12:23,953
and unites them?

188
00:12:25,173 --> 00:12:28,276
We're not just looking for some sort of notional characteristic,

189
00:12:29,357 --> 00:12:32,939
some quality that we can say they all have in common.

190
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:37,443
What we're really looking for, Heidegger is going to steer us towards.

191
00:12:39,844 --> 00:12:42,086
Here's giving away some of the answers.

192
00:12:43,205 --> 00:12:46,047
is in large part the human comportment

193
00:12:47,367 --> 00:12:50,549
and the human making meaning out of technology.

194
00:12:51,589 --> 00:12:52,790
So let's see where he goes.

195
00:12:52,850 --> 00:12:53,330
He says,

196
00:12:55,191 --> 00:13:01,214
we will never experience our relationship to the essence of technology so long as we merely

197
00:13:01,314 --> 00:13:03,415
represent and pursue the technological.

198
00:13:04,516 --> 00:13:06,917
So long as we remain within a certain

199
00:13:07,638 --> 00:13:12,360
attitude towards technology where we're comparing pieces of technology against each other,

200
00:13:12,754 --> 00:13:19,456
where we're playing with it, where we're using it, where we're becoming more and more obsessed

201
00:13:19,516 --> 00:13:22,117
with it, where we read reports about it, where we

202
00:13:22,857 --> 00:13:28,099
troubleshoot it. We're not actually engaging with the essence of technology.

203
00:13:28,639 --> 00:13:29,879
Likewise, he says,

204
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:33,500
if we only put up with it, we're evaded.

205
00:13:34,361 --> 00:13:40,963
So it's not just overly positive attitudes towards technology that keep

206
00:13:41,083 --> 00:13:42,143
people from being able to

207
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:44,540
the essence of it,

208
00:13:45,521 --> 00:13:46,201
and thereby have

209
00:13:46,982 --> 00:13:50,805
this free relationship of their existence towards technology.

210
00:13:52,186 --> 00:13:53,487
It's also the

211
00:13:54,747 --> 00:14:00,792
viewing it as something that just has to be endured, that has to be put up with, that one has

212
00:14:00,852 --> 00:14:04,534
to come to terms with. Or the rejection of it.

213
00:14:05,375 --> 00:14:08,757
The saying, technology, it's all bad.

214
00:14:09,098 --> 00:14:12,580
We need to get back to the garden, or pick whatever hippie trope that you like.

215
00:14:14,128 --> 00:14:18,752
or Rousseauian trooper, Emersonian trooper, you know, who's been around for quite a while,

216
00:14:18,772 --> 00:14:19,473
this sentiment.

217
00:14:21,755 --> 00:14:22,555
None of those

218
00:14:23,536 --> 00:14:28,100
really allow us access to the essence of technology.

219
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:32,923
None of those help us understand what technology in itself is.

220
00:14:34,004 --> 00:14:34,565
So he says,

221
00:14:35,486 --> 00:14:39,269
everywhere we've remained free and chained to technology, whether we passionately affirm or

222
00:14:39,289 --> 00:14:39,729
deny it,

223
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:43,742
but we're delivered over to it in the worst possible way

224
00:14:44,602 --> 00:14:47,604
when we regard it as something neutral.

225
00:14:49,045 --> 00:14:49,545
There is this

226
00:14:50,345 --> 00:14:52,547
common notion that

227
00:14:53,407 --> 00:15:00,051
to understand technology, we should be value-free and merely understand

228
00:15:00,091 --> 00:15:06,794
technology as something that can be used for good or bad or efficiency or non-efficiency,

229
00:15:07,155 --> 00:15:09,656
that it carries no values with it.

230
00:15:10,342 --> 00:15:11,843
It doesn't steer us towards anything.

231
00:15:11,943 --> 00:15:16,586
It's a pure means to be used by human beings however they like.

232
00:15:18,087 --> 00:15:23,991
That's not true. Every time that we introduce technology, we change our trajectory to a certain

233
00:15:24,031 --> 00:15:24,431
extent.

234
00:15:26,613 --> 00:15:27,213
So he says,

235
00:15:30,876 --> 00:15:33,958
according to ancient doctrine, if we want to talk about metaphysics,

236
00:15:34,898 --> 00:15:37,800
The essence of a thing is considered to be what the thing is.

237
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:41,281
We ask the question concerning technology when we ask what it is.

238
00:15:41,441 --> 00:15:43,202
So that seems pretty straightforward, doesn't it?

239
00:15:44,203 --> 00:15:47,424
One says technology is a means to an end.

240
00:15:50,226 --> 00:15:54,288
Another says technology is a human activity.

241
00:15:54,628 --> 00:15:55,369
And Heidegger says,

242
00:15:56,629 --> 00:15:57,930
do we have to choose between these?

243
00:15:57,990 --> 00:16:01,712
No, they're both right. Technology is in fact a means to an end.

244
00:16:01,872 --> 00:16:03,993
I write on the chalkboard in order to convey...

245
00:16:05,192 --> 00:16:09,035
words which are supposed to convey thoughts to you.

246
00:16:09,375 --> 00:16:12,978
And I use another piece of technology, this camera, in order to do that.

247
00:16:12,998 --> 00:16:15,040
And then I'll be uploading it on my computer.

248
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:17,702
I'm gesturing over there because that's where it happens to be right now.

249
00:16:18,903 --> 00:16:23,206
And while I'm doing that, I'll be sitting in my office chair, which has been adjusted to the

250
00:16:23,306 --> 00:16:28,270
proper fit for my body. And the place will be illuminated by electric lights.

251
00:16:29,351 --> 00:16:35,092
My computer will be charging while I While I do that, I'll be plugging this flip cam into it.

252
00:16:35,692 --> 00:16:38,794
All these things are means to certain ends.

253
00:16:38,974 --> 00:16:41,416
And they're also human activities.

254
00:16:41,436 --> 00:16:42,397
They're the product

255
00:16:43,117 --> 00:16:43,238
of

256
00:16:43,958 --> 00:16:46,440
human ingenuity, of innovation, of

257
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:48,642
making things out of

258
00:16:49,402 --> 00:16:49,943
other things.

259
00:16:51,704 --> 00:16:53,865
So he says, these two come together.

260
00:16:54,786 --> 00:16:55,807
Why? Well, because...

261
00:16:57,043 --> 00:17:01,906
Positing means for ends is the distinctively human activity.

262
00:17:03,407 --> 00:17:05,109
That's part of what we are.

263
00:17:05,269 --> 00:17:08,391
We are beings that actually say, I want to do this.

264
00:17:08,751 --> 00:17:10,673
How do I make that happen?

265
00:17:11,313 --> 00:17:14,095
What things do I need to do along the way?

266
00:17:16,216 --> 00:17:21,880
These go together. So technology becomes what Heidegger calls an instrumentum, an instrument,

267
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:23,121
a tool, an aid.

268
00:17:24,562 --> 00:17:25,403
And he says...

269
00:17:27,867 --> 00:17:29,527
Who would ever deny that this is correct?

270
00:17:30,968 --> 00:17:34,608
It is an obvious conformity with what we're envisaging when we talk about technology.

271
00:17:35,008 --> 00:17:38,689
This is where a lot of conversations about technology actually begin.

272
00:17:38,709 --> 00:17:43,950
It says the instrumental definition of technology is indeed so uncannily correct that it even

273
00:17:44,030 --> 00:17:45,911
holds for modern technology.

274
00:17:46,271 --> 00:17:48,451
Not only holds for ancient technology,

275
00:17:49,411 --> 00:17:54,992
like say a mortar and pestle, it holds for as modern technology as the

276
00:17:55,232 --> 00:17:57,713
iPhone or the computer network

277
00:17:58,520 --> 00:17:59,760
or the banking system.

278
00:18:00,921 --> 00:18:01,521
So he says,

279
00:18:04,241 --> 00:18:10,063
even the power plant with its turbines and generators is a man-made means to an end established

280
00:18:10,123 --> 00:18:14,824
by man. Even the jet aircraft and high-frequency apparatus are means to ends.

281
00:18:15,424 --> 00:18:18,885
A radar station is, of course, less simple than a weather vane.

282
00:18:20,325 --> 00:18:20,585
But

283
00:18:23,423 --> 00:18:24,664
This much remains correct.

284
00:18:24,844 --> 00:18:26,905
Modern technology, too, is a means to an end.

285
00:18:26,985 --> 00:18:31,268
That is why the instrumental conception of technology conditions, this is an important thing,

286
00:18:32,089 --> 00:18:36,712
conditions every attempt to try to understand technology.

287
00:18:37,332 --> 00:18:44,197
When we begin our discussions of technology, this is the de facto or default

288
00:18:44,437 --> 00:18:47,859
position that people begin with in mind.

289
00:18:49,260 --> 00:18:51,621
And is it correct? Yes, it is correct.

290
00:18:52,919 --> 00:18:54,020
Is it adequate?

291
00:18:54,781 --> 00:18:55,701
It's not adequate.

292
00:18:56,422 --> 00:18:57,703
It doesn't give us what we need.

293
00:18:57,723 --> 00:18:58,604
It doesn't tell us the

294
00:19:00,586 --> 00:19:01,667
essence. He says,

295
00:19:03,428 --> 00:19:06,731
everything depends on our manipulating technology and the proper manners and means.

296
00:19:07,211 --> 00:19:10,154
We will, as we say, get technology intelligently in hand.

297
00:19:10,174 --> 00:19:15,178
We will master it. And the will to mastery, he says, becomes all the more urgent the more technology

298
00:19:15,218 --> 00:19:17,100
threatens to slip from human control.

299
00:19:18,273 --> 00:19:23,295
So, you know, think now for just a contemporary example that Heidegger didn't have access to.

300
00:19:24,075 --> 00:19:30,877
Think about all these people on their phones spending all this time and the technology

301
00:19:30,977 --> 00:19:35,599
that can be used, which actually you can upload as an app on your phone to tell you, hey, knock

302
00:19:35,699 --> 00:19:37,599
off playing around on your phone.

303
00:19:37,619 --> 00:19:40,280
You've spent X amount of hours on it so far.

304
00:19:40,660 --> 00:19:45,002
You need to actually do some real life, you know, this is putting it in quotes because

305
00:19:45,925 --> 00:19:47,526
Working on your phone is real life, too.

306
00:19:47,586 --> 00:19:49,727
It's just a smaller part of the real life.

307
00:19:50,347 --> 00:19:53,049
You need to get back to all these other things.

308
00:19:54,410 --> 00:19:58,792
We use technology to try to control technology, which has gotten out of hand.

309
00:19:59,152 --> 00:20:03,234
And all of that is reflective of what Heidegger calls a will to mastery.

310
00:20:04,795 --> 00:20:07,677
Now, here's where we're going to start making our transition.

311
00:20:07,737 --> 00:20:08,117
He says,

312
00:20:09,758 --> 00:20:13,020
suppose now that technology were no mere means.

313
00:20:14,247 --> 00:20:16,188
that this is actually incorrect.

314
00:20:17,108 --> 00:20:19,910
How would it stand with the will to master it?

315
00:20:21,970 --> 00:20:26,713
And he says, wait, didn't we just say that this notion of it as a means was correct?

316
00:20:26,733 --> 00:20:28,293
And he says, yeah, it's correct.

317
00:20:29,494 --> 00:20:34,116
However, in order to be correct, this fixing by no means needs to uncover the thing in question

318
00:20:34,176 --> 00:20:39,858
in its essence. Only at the point where such an uncovering happens does the true come out,

319
00:20:39,999 --> 00:20:40,819
Heidegger says.

320
00:20:42,139 --> 00:20:43,120
We're only really...

321
00:20:43,920 --> 00:20:47,083
fully aware of what's going on with our technology

322
00:20:48,043 --> 00:20:50,846
when we can get away from this

323
00:20:51,787 --> 00:20:52,367
correct

324
00:20:53,848 --> 00:20:56,010
but inadequate conception.

325
00:20:57,051 --> 00:21:00,394
And what is going to be our access point for that is going to be

326
00:21:01,274 --> 00:21:02,736
getting away from mere

327
00:21:03,496 --> 00:21:05,838
praxis, or you might say a

328
00:21:06,579 --> 00:21:12,964
physics of the apparent, to metaphysics, thinking about what really is going on here.

329
00:21:16,286 --> 00:21:16,566
So,

330
00:21:17,267 --> 00:21:17,747
he says,

331
00:21:19,829 --> 00:21:24,112
only at the point where such an uncovering happens do we find the true.

332
00:21:24,152 --> 00:21:26,354
For that reason, the merely correct is not the true.

333
00:21:27,454 --> 00:21:29,576
There's a difference between the

334
00:21:33,079 --> 00:21:34,980
true and the correct.

335
00:21:37,122 --> 00:21:41,605
Accordingly, the correct instrumental definition of technology still does not show us technology's

336
00:21:41,725 --> 00:21:46,162
essence. In order that we may arrive at this, We must seek the true by the way of the correct.

337
00:21:46,182 --> 00:21:46,742
We must ask,

338
00:21:47,583 --> 00:21:49,443
what is the instrumental?

339
00:21:55,526 --> 00:21:56,947
We have to make that into a question.

340
00:21:58,948 --> 00:22:00,949
What is the instrumental?

341
00:22:03,670 --> 00:22:04,451
We ask that.

342
00:22:05,791 --> 00:22:08,993
We place ourselves on the road to actually understand what is technology.

343
00:22:09,673 --> 00:22:13,015
Within what do such things as means and end belong?

344
00:22:14,529 --> 00:22:20,072
So he says, wherever, whatever has an effect as its consequence is called a cause.

345
00:22:20,992 --> 00:22:24,354
A means is that whereby something is affected and thus attained.

346
00:22:24,734 --> 00:22:28,436
So we're moving from just means and ends to talk about

347
00:22:31,178 --> 00:22:33,199
causality, cause and effect.

348
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:36,781
Something deeper.

349
00:22:38,282 --> 00:22:38,842
So he says,

350
00:22:39,605 --> 00:22:43,806
The end that determines the kind of means to be used may also be considered a cause.

351
00:22:43,946 --> 00:22:45,346
Aristotle talked about this.

352
00:22:45,466 --> 00:22:47,067
Plato actually hinted at this.

353
00:22:47,467 --> 00:22:48,727
They call this a final cause.

354
00:22:49,327 --> 00:22:54,269
This is wherever means and ends are employed, wherever instrumentality reigns, there reigns

355
00:22:54,649 --> 00:22:55,489
causality.

356
00:22:57,069 --> 00:23:01,530
This is where he's going to move now to talk about the four causes.

357
00:23:02,350 --> 00:23:06,211
So we want to pause for a moment and just think about where we've come so far.

358
00:23:07,512 --> 00:23:09,603
We've gotten away... from

359
00:23:10,524 --> 00:23:13,446
being enraptured or being ensorcelled or being

360
00:23:14,247 --> 00:23:15,568
bewildered by

361
00:23:16,709 --> 00:23:20,192
thinking that this is an adequate understanding of technology.

362
00:23:20,272 --> 00:23:23,815
We've realized that if we want to genuinely pose the question

363
00:23:24,976 --> 00:23:25,116
of

364
00:23:25,816 --> 00:23:27,077
what is technology?

365
00:23:27,978 --> 00:23:32,622
How does it really work? How does it really interact with human beings?

366
00:23:33,242 --> 00:23:35,544
Not just essentially, but existentially.

367
00:23:36,255 --> 00:23:39,116
We have to move now to the realm of metaphysics.

368
00:23:41,717 --> 00:23:45,999
Wherever instrumentality reigns, there is causality.

369
00:23:46,439 --> 00:23:48,740
That's one of the things that Heidegger has said so far.

370
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:55,262
What does that mean? So he's going to now explore this in terms of what may be familiar to

371
00:23:55,522 --> 00:23:56,083
many of you

372
00:23:56,823 --> 00:23:59,704
as the four clauses that we get from Aristotle.

373
00:24:00,344 --> 00:24:04,946
And these are sort of scholastic ways of talking about them that Heidegger uses in this.

374
00:24:05,634 --> 00:24:11,738
He uses the example of a silver chalice, which we'll come to momentarily, and he says that

375
00:24:14,299 --> 00:24:17,101
for centuries philosophy has taught there are four causes.

376
00:24:17,701 --> 00:24:18,061
The

377
00:24:19,622 --> 00:24:21,663
causa materialis, the material,

378
00:24:22,384 --> 00:24:22,604
the

379
00:24:23,544 --> 00:24:25,926
matter out of which a silver chalice is made.

380
00:24:26,346 --> 00:24:30,668
The causa formalis, the form, the shape into which the material enters, the

381
00:24:31,389 --> 00:24:33,250
shape, the structure of it.

382
00:24:33,815 --> 00:24:36,057
The causa finalis, the

383
00:24:37,178 --> 00:24:42,182
reason, the end, for example, the sacrificial rite in relation to which the required chalice

384
00:24:42,662 --> 00:24:44,704
is determined as to its form in matter.

385
00:24:44,744 --> 00:24:51,209
So notice that already causa finalis is binding these together.

386
00:24:52,089 --> 00:24:55,492
It's not just a matter of form being imposed on matter.

387
00:24:56,273 --> 00:24:58,254
The reason, the end, is important.

388
00:24:58,394 --> 00:25:03,732
And then he says the causa officiens, which brings about the effect that is the finished actual

389
00:25:03,772 --> 00:25:05,873
chalice, in this case the silversmith.

390
00:25:06,013 --> 00:25:07,755
So we have the traditional

391
00:25:08,475 --> 00:25:09,115
conception,

392
00:25:10,016 --> 00:25:10,496
silver,

393
00:25:11,557 --> 00:25:12,378
chalice shape,

394
00:25:13,358 --> 00:25:15,500
sacrificial rite, silversmith.

395
00:25:16,801 --> 00:25:18,221
We can do this with many other things.

396
00:25:19,202 --> 00:25:19,622
Baker,

397
00:25:20,803 --> 00:25:22,064
flour, water, yeast,

398
00:25:23,825 --> 00:25:26,007
loaf, I suppose you could say that the

399
00:25:27,188 --> 00:25:29,929
Baking of it is also a way of introducing form to it.

400
00:25:30,890 --> 00:25:33,871
And perhaps the reason is to make money.

401
00:25:33,911 --> 00:25:36,033
Perhaps the reason is to gratify

402
00:25:36,753 --> 00:25:38,634
the palate and the belly at the same time.

403
00:25:39,094 --> 00:25:42,516
Perhaps the reason is to make bread for the king.

404
00:25:42,876 --> 00:25:45,457
Who knows? There could be a lot of different reasons here.

405
00:25:46,418 --> 00:25:47,218
The point is,

406
00:25:48,359 --> 00:25:50,420
we have for a long time

407
00:25:51,220 --> 00:25:52,641
looked at causality

408
00:25:53,382 --> 00:25:55,943
in this way. So Heidegger then says,

409
00:25:57,893 --> 00:26:03,815
What technology is, when represented as a means, discloses itself when we trace instrumentality

410
00:26:03,955 --> 00:26:06,375
back to this fourfold causality.

411
00:26:07,896 --> 00:26:12,077
Well, why? Well, when it comes to technology, we can ask, what is it made of?

412
00:26:13,077 --> 00:26:15,277
Think of a pliers, for example.

413
00:26:15,337 --> 00:26:16,418
Very simple technology.

414
00:26:16,898 --> 00:26:18,278
What is it made of? Steel.

415
00:26:20,118 --> 00:26:22,359
What is the form? The shape of the pliers.

416
00:26:23,084 --> 00:26:27,667
And if you were to adequately describe that, you would have to talk about the gripping surface

417
00:26:27,967 --> 00:26:32,791
and how many teeth there are and the little nut that you have to hold them together.

418
00:26:34,012 --> 00:26:35,313
Probably you'd talk about the

419
00:26:38,395 --> 00:26:42,338
covering or the rough surface on the handle so you could use it.

420
00:26:42,938 --> 00:26:46,841
What is the final cause of it so that you can grip things even better?

421
00:26:47,361 --> 00:26:52,725
Notice that it's a piece of technology that allows you to do things with technology.

422
00:26:53,385 --> 00:26:58,410
And then what's the cause of efficients, the efficient cause, the producing cause, the factory

423
00:26:58,450 --> 00:27:03,235
that produced the pliers perhaps, a Sears craftsman, you know, company,

424
00:27:04,797 --> 00:27:10,243
the individual person who operated the die press, and then, you know, the one who assembled

425
00:27:10,283 --> 00:27:10,863
the things.

426
00:27:11,564 --> 00:27:14,247
All these can be understood in that way.

427
00:27:15,565 --> 00:27:21,010
And the process by which all these causes are integrated with each other is a more complex

428
00:27:21,170 --> 00:27:24,393
one, but this allows us a schema by which we can make sense of it.

429
00:27:25,193 --> 00:27:26,174
So Heidegger says,

430
00:27:28,136 --> 00:27:32,439
suppose that causality, for its part, is veiled in darkness with respect to what it is.

431
00:27:34,201 --> 00:27:35,882
Let's say things are not as simple as this.

432
00:27:36,743 --> 00:27:40,246
And I don't mean that we can complicate the picture more.

433
00:27:41,127 --> 00:27:42,688
That's not what Heidegger means either.

434
00:27:43,207 --> 00:27:46,789
What he's talking about when he's saying perhaps it's not so simple as this

435
00:27:47,810 --> 00:27:51,372
is that perhaps these things are not what we took them to be.

436
00:27:53,093 --> 00:27:58,076
He says, We've acted as if the doctrine of four causes had fallen from heaven as a truth as

437
00:27:58,116 --> 00:28:03,000
clear as daylight, but might it be that the time has come to ask, why are there only four causes?

438
00:28:04,881 --> 00:28:06,442
It's a good question. Why four?

439
00:28:06,462 --> 00:28:06,662
Why four?

440
00:28:07,709 --> 00:28:11,031
in relation to the aforementioned four, what does cause really mean?

441
00:28:11,251 --> 00:28:12,892
What is the essence of cause

442
00:28:14,032 --> 00:28:15,313
that connects all of these together?

443
00:28:16,434 --> 00:28:20,236
And notice what he's going to say next, and I'm not going to read it directly, I'm going to

444
00:28:20,256 --> 00:28:21,897
just sort of summarize it for you.

445
00:28:24,718 --> 00:28:25,899
Not only in modernity,

446
00:28:26,999 --> 00:28:29,040
but it's become more pronounced in modernity,

447
00:28:30,781 --> 00:28:34,423
but even in ancient times, there was a tendency to make this one

448
00:28:38,259 --> 00:28:43,301
into the cause of causes, the paradigm for causes.

449
00:28:43,981 --> 00:28:49,763
If you look at modern philosophy, you will see this, at least certain modern philosophy, like

450
00:28:49,783 --> 00:28:50,863
Descartes, for example,

451
00:28:52,484 --> 00:28:55,905
you will see this becoming very, very starkly clear.

452
00:28:56,505 --> 00:29:02,287
And in some of Descartes' successors, like Leibniz, Leibniz is trying to bring back final causes

453
00:29:02,507 --> 00:29:09,286
into this schema. because the Cartesians had tried to reduce everything, basically, to

454
00:29:09,306 --> 00:29:10,947
deficient causes,

455
00:29:11,968 --> 00:29:15,149
causing things to take on a certain form or shape,

456
00:29:16,590 --> 00:29:17,651
and what they were made of.

457
00:29:22,273 --> 00:29:25,835
So he says the causa efficiens sets the standard for all causality.

458
00:29:27,295 --> 00:29:32,198
This goes so far we no longer even count this last one, telefinality, as causality.

459
00:29:34,019 --> 00:29:34,159
And

460
00:29:34,925 --> 00:29:38,607
Heidegger wants to get at something different, which is actually going to make this one more

461
00:29:38,687 --> 00:29:39,668
important. He says,

462
00:29:40,488 --> 00:29:42,489
what does cause really mean?

463
00:29:46,811 --> 00:29:47,212
He says,

464
00:29:49,293 --> 00:29:50,814
if we're looking at it

465
00:29:52,134 --> 00:29:53,915
in certain Latin terms,

466
00:29:55,716 --> 00:29:59,158
causa, casus, belongs to the verb cadre, to fall.

467
00:29:59,739 --> 00:30:02,500
And it has to do with why things turn out in a certain way.

468
00:30:03,857 --> 00:30:06,319
So he says, the doctrine of the four causes goes back to Aristotle.

469
00:30:06,339 --> 00:30:09,422
But everything that later ages seek in Greek thought under the conception

470
00:30:10,643 --> 00:30:13,386
has nothing to do at all with bringing about and affecting.

471
00:30:13,406 --> 00:30:16,229
What we call cause, in German, Ursache,

472
00:30:17,029 --> 00:30:19,812
and the Romans call causa, is called Aetion,

473
00:30:23,135 --> 00:30:23,716
by the Greeks.

474
00:30:25,557 --> 00:30:26,578
What does that actually mean?

475
00:30:27,559 --> 00:30:29,281
That to which something is indebted.

476
00:30:30,317 --> 00:30:36,860
The four causes, he says, are all ways, all belonging at once to each other, of being responsible

477
00:30:37,340 --> 00:30:39,902
to something else. So these are interconnected

478
00:30:40,962 --> 00:30:41,743
with each other.

479
00:30:42,703 --> 00:30:44,364
And they're all ways of

480
00:30:49,326 --> 00:30:50,487
being responsible.

481
00:30:53,857 --> 00:30:58,479
That may strike you as a rather anthropomorphic way of talking about things.

482
00:30:58,819 --> 00:31:00,520
If you think about the silver chalice,

483
00:31:02,761 --> 00:31:08,663
the metal, the silver itself, is responsible for the being of

484
00:31:09,703 --> 00:31:16,366
the chalice, for it being the kind of thing that it is, being made of silver, having the characteristics

485
00:31:16,406 --> 00:31:18,047
that it does because of that material.

486
00:31:18,307 --> 00:31:18,687
He says,

487
00:31:19,927 --> 00:31:22,522
it is co-responsible. for the chalice.

488
00:31:22,822 --> 00:31:24,463
The chalice is indebted to,

489
00:31:25,304 --> 00:31:30,488
that is owes thanks to, the silver for that of which it consists.

490
00:31:31,909 --> 00:31:38,073
But the sacrificial vessel is indebted not only to the silver, as a chalice, that which is

491
00:31:38,133 --> 00:31:43,317
indebted to the silver appears in the aspect of a chalice, in its form, in its structure, in

492
00:31:43,377 --> 00:31:44,918
its shape, in its

493
00:31:45,779 --> 00:31:46,940
eidos in Greek.

494
00:31:49,213 --> 00:31:52,916
Why is it a chalice and not, he says, a brooch or a ring?

495
00:31:54,156 --> 00:31:57,699
Why an iPhone and not just a whole bunch of parts all thrown together?

496
00:31:58,479 --> 00:32:00,401
Because it has a structure.

497
00:32:01,642 --> 00:32:02,662
Why a cup

498
00:32:04,444 --> 00:32:07,806
and not a cylinder that has a hole at the bottom?

499
00:32:08,627 --> 00:32:08,847
Well,

500
00:32:11,388 --> 00:32:13,590
it wouldn't be much of a cup if it had that, would it?

501
00:32:14,891 --> 00:32:17,953
So he says both of these are responsible for it.

502
00:32:18,955 --> 00:32:23,599
Then he says, there remains yet a third something that is above all responsible for that sacrificial

503
00:32:23,659 --> 00:32:29,265
vessel. It is that which in advance confines the chalice within the realm of consecration and

504
00:32:29,305 --> 00:32:29,925
bestowal.

505
00:32:30,726 --> 00:32:34,169
Through this, the chalice is circumscribed as sacrificial vessel.

506
00:32:34,690 --> 00:32:37,572
Circumscribing gives balance to the thing.

507
00:32:38,113 --> 00:32:42,196
So not only do we have form being imposed on matter,

508
00:32:42,917 --> 00:32:44,819
why is form imposed on matter?

509
00:32:48,840 --> 00:32:50,441
Because the final cause,

510
00:32:52,181 --> 00:32:52,362
which

511
00:32:53,442 --> 00:32:58,184
really isn't a final cause in the sense of temporality, it precedes the whole

512
00:32:59,204 --> 00:33:02,026
working, the whole manufacturing process.

513
00:33:02,126 --> 00:33:04,387
The final cause, the reason for

514
00:33:05,127 --> 00:33:09,449
the chalice being made out of silver and made as a chalice

515
00:33:11,129 --> 00:33:16,772
is the rituals that it's going to be used for, the meaning that it possesses for human beings.

516
00:33:18,817 --> 00:33:21,318
So this is, in a certain sense, more important,

517
00:33:22,158 --> 00:33:24,779
more causal, you might say.

518
00:33:25,620 --> 00:33:26,220
So he says,

519
00:33:29,401 --> 00:33:34,683
finally, there's a fourth participant in the responsibility for the finished sacrificial vessels

520
00:33:34,763 --> 00:33:36,284
lying before us ready to use,

521
00:33:37,344 --> 00:33:38,204
the silversmith.

522
00:33:39,485 --> 00:33:40,445
What is the silversmith?

523
00:33:40,485 --> 00:33:43,326
The silversmith is a worker who,

524
00:33:44,327 --> 00:33:45,927
for the reasons over here,

525
00:33:46,707 --> 00:33:50,345
imparts the form to the material,

526
00:33:51,145 --> 00:33:52,886
and thereby produces a chalice.

527
00:33:54,546 --> 00:33:55,186
So he says,

528
00:33:57,127 --> 00:34:02,007
the silversmith, but not at all because he in working brings about the finished sacrificial

529
00:34:02,047 --> 00:34:03,888
chalices if it were the effect of a making.

530
00:34:04,328 --> 00:34:08,008
The silversmith is actually not a causa efficiens.

531
00:34:09,269 --> 00:34:09,649
Why? He

532
00:34:10,709 --> 00:34:14,970
says, the Aristotelian doctrine neither knows the cause that is named by this term nor uses

533
00:34:15,030 --> 00:34:16,550
a Greek word that would correspond to it.

534
00:34:16,937 --> 00:34:19,098
So Heidegger is introducing something new here.

535
00:34:20,539 --> 00:34:21,719
Let's put the silversmith.

536
00:34:24,841 --> 00:34:26,081
Silversmith is a

537
00:34:28,242 --> 00:34:31,283
maker, and he's not the same thing as the causa efficiens.

538
00:34:32,764 --> 00:34:33,204
Why not?

539
00:34:34,565 --> 00:34:39,107
Because it's not so simple or so reducible or so direct as that.

540
00:34:40,127 --> 00:34:41,528
He's involved in something.

541
00:34:42,268 --> 00:34:47,792
He says the silversmith considers carefully and gathers together the three aforementioned ways

542
00:34:47,832 --> 00:34:49,732
of being responsible and indebted.

543
00:34:50,252 --> 00:34:54,374
So the silversmith is gathering in all of this.

544
00:34:55,754 --> 00:34:58,475
The form, the matter, the final end.

545
00:35:00,456 --> 00:35:03,957
To consider carefully is in Greek, legein,

546
00:35:04,737 --> 00:35:07,418
from which we get logos. We're actually the opposite, too.

547
00:35:07,938 --> 00:35:10,139
Legein is rooted in apophynosthai.

548
00:35:10,279 --> 00:35:11,219
Apophynosthai

549
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:15,901
is a word that means to bring forth, to show.

550
00:35:17,394 --> 00:35:17,854
to,

551
00:35:18,755 --> 00:35:21,176
in some ways, set before the gaze,

552
00:35:22,037 --> 00:35:28,700
before the visibility of others, to bring into existence, to bring into being.

553
00:35:30,241 --> 00:35:34,564
The silversmith is co-responsible as that from which the sacred vessels being brought forth

554
00:35:34,624 --> 00:35:39,226
in subsistence take and retain their first departure, he says.

555
00:35:40,167 --> 00:35:41,928
So, what's going on here?

556
00:35:42,338 --> 00:35:47,859
Heidegger is revealed to us by questioning, by carrying on this process of

557
00:35:48,599 --> 00:35:54,460
metaphysical examination of what's actually going on using an example of a

558
00:35:54,540 --> 00:36:00,581
silversmith, a worker, a shaper of an object that is a form of technology.

559
00:36:01,902 --> 00:36:07,163
He has shown us that things are not as simple with the causality schema as we thought they

560
00:36:07,183 --> 00:36:08,823
were. The four causes

561
00:36:10,400 --> 00:36:12,601
actually are not all on the same level.

562
00:36:13,462 --> 00:36:18,865
And the silversmith himself doesn't fit adequately into this schema of the four causes.

563
00:36:20,806 --> 00:36:21,426
So he says,

564
00:36:26,369 --> 00:36:30,451
Four ways of owing hold sway in the sacrificial vessel that lies before us.

565
00:36:30,811 --> 00:36:33,512
They differ from each other, yet they belong together.

566
00:36:34,033 --> 00:36:35,994
What unites them from the beginning?

567
00:36:36,894 --> 00:36:38,455
Now we're going to a deeper question.

568
00:36:39,663 --> 00:36:43,526
in what does this playing in unison of the four ways of being responsible play?

569
00:36:44,526 --> 00:36:46,628
What is the source of the unity of the four causes?

570
00:36:47,128 --> 00:36:50,110
Where, after all, does the owing and being responsible mean?

571
00:36:50,590 --> 00:36:52,371
Thought as the Greeks thought it.

572
00:36:54,133 --> 00:37:00,076
He says, today we're too inclined, when we hear the word responsibility or being responsible,

573
00:37:00,096 --> 00:37:03,739
to think about it in ways that are going to lead us astray.

574
00:37:03,939 --> 00:37:05,400
So he talks about

575
00:37:06,223 --> 00:37:10,665
as owing, like having done something wrong, having

576
00:37:11,545 --> 00:37:12,625
let somebody down, as

577
00:37:14,586 --> 00:37:15,486
a lapse,

578
00:37:16,547 --> 00:37:18,908
or we construe them in terms of affecting.

579
00:37:18,948 --> 00:37:22,429
And he says, in either case, we borrow from ourselves the weight of the primal meaning of the

580
00:37:22,489 --> 00:37:25,130
word, the metaphysics of it.

581
00:37:26,770 --> 00:37:33,073
Here now, Heidegger is going to make a very important move which connects this discussion of

582
00:37:33,093 --> 00:37:34,013
the four clauses

583
00:37:35,123 --> 00:37:37,324
and of technology, and of producing,

584
00:37:38,064 --> 00:37:44,887
with some of his other notions that he has elaborated in other works, such as Being in Time,

585
00:37:44,907 --> 00:37:51,590
but also what is metaphysics, the nature of truth, all sorts of, well, also the

586
00:37:53,371 --> 00:37:55,072
discussions of the work of art.

587
00:37:56,152 --> 00:38:00,374
What's going on here is Heidegger is now going to illuminate

588
00:38:02,195 --> 00:38:04,616
what happens when we make something.

589
00:38:05,482 --> 00:38:11,926
So he says that we can understand these four causes now in terms of four ways of being

590
00:38:11,986 --> 00:38:12,687
responsible.

591
00:38:14,087 --> 00:38:15,688
And this is the language that he uses.

592
00:38:16,349 --> 00:38:17,790
He talks about occasioning.

593
00:38:20,631 --> 00:38:27,316
And he says that we have to be very careful not to get misled by other uses of this term occasioning.

594
00:38:27,336 --> 00:38:30,117
He means something very broad, something metaphysical, something

595
00:38:30,978 --> 00:38:34,300
that has to do with the nature of being and activity itself.

596
00:38:34,868 --> 00:38:36,809
So he says, these four ways,

597
00:38:37,510 --> 00:38:40,212
what they do is they bring something into appearance.

598
00:38:41,933 --> 00:38:43,374
What does he mean by appearance there?

599
00:38:43,394 --> 00:38:45,816
Just the superficial outside of the thing?

600
00:38:46,557 --> 00:38:53,122
No, he means that they allow things to actually appear as things for things that

601
00:38:53,202 --> 00:38:54,062
perceive them

602
00:38:55,043 --> 00:38:57,505
as things. That is, now that

603
00:38:58,285 --> 00:39:00,667
I've probably confused you a little bit with all this thing language,

604
00:39:02,308 --> 00:39:04,330
when something comes into appearance,

605
00:39:05,095 --> 00:39:11,880
it shows four human subjects, four human beings, which are able to see those things as

606
00:39:12,300 --> 00:39:14,262
objects, which are able to

607
00:39:15,663 --> 00:39:22,487
imbue them with meaning, to allow meaning to accrue to them, to make sense of them,

608
00:39:22,607 --> 00:39:25,369
to make use of them, to talk about them.

609
00:39:27,811 --> 00:39:31,814
That's something that's involved in this bringing into appearance.

610
00:39:31,854 --> 00:39:34,596
So when the silversmith makes the chalice,

611
00:39:36,125 --> 00:39:43,008
He doesn't merely take matter, which then he stamps a form onto and says, there you go,

612
00:39:43,028 --> 00:39:44,989
put it in the package and get it the hell out of here.

613
00:39:46,590 --> 00:39:48,591
He is bringing something into being.

614
00:39:50,652 --> 00:39:54,354
He is making something that human beings can then look at and say,

615
00:39:55,094 --> 00:39:55,594
aha,

616
00:39:56,335 --> 00:39:57,215
that's a chalice.

617
00:39:57,973 --> 00:40:01,015
Or they can say, ah, I don't know what the hell that is.

618
00:40:01,115 --> 00:40:04,238
What is it? And they can be provoked to question about it.

619
00:40:04,278 --> 00:40:06,980
They can mistake it. Even the possibility of being able to mistake it

620
00:40:07,961 --> 00:40:10,102
relies on it being brought into appearance.

621
00:40:11,003 --> 00:40:17,768
Heidegger also says that this work of occasioning, these causes, they let the

622
00:40:17,808 --> 00:40:20,050
thing come forth into presence.

623
00:40:20,630 --> 00:40:21,931
He uses language here,

624
00:40:22,792 --> 00:40:27,395
which is not so much the language of agency, like pushing, pushing, pushing,

625
00:40:28,363 --> 00:40:31,384
but rather cajoling, saying, come on,

626
00:40:32,345 --> 00:40:36,927
come on, come on with me. You want to be, you want to exist.

627
00:40:37,927 --> 00:40:39,108
Now here's your chance.

628
00:40:41,169 --> 00:40:45,390
Heidegger's saying this about the silver, he's saying this about the form, he's saying this

629
00:40:45,450 --> 00:40:49,752
about the purpose, the final end, and he's saying this about the maker or shaper

630
00:40:50,473 --> 00:40:51,713
or technologer.

631
00:40:53,374 --> 00:40:54,874
So it What else does he say?

632
00:40:55,014 --> 00:41:00,077
He uses this language, beautiful language, of actually setting the thing free.

633
00:41:00,578 --> 00:41:02,619
Starting it down on its way.

634
00:41:02,639 --> 00:41:06,082
I'm going to read you the passage because it's so wonderful.

635
00:41:07,403 --> 00:41:09,204
The four ways of being responsible.

636
00:41:09,364 --> 00:41:14,187
Set it free to that place and so start it on its way, namely into its complete arrival.

637
00:41:16,729 --> 00:41:18,571
Think about that for a moment.

638
00:41:19,811 --> 00:41:20,672
And think about the

639
00:41:21,673 --> 00:41:23,334
artistic, the creative...

640
00:41:24,105 --> 00:41:24,745
the even

641
00:41:25,686 --> 00:41:32,649
workaday manufacturing process, as allowing something to be what it's going to be,

642
00:41:32,829 --> 00:41:37,251
as allowing it to assume the shape and the purpose

643
00:41:38,072 --> 00:41:42,714
that lies latent, dormant within it as potentiality.

644
00:41:43,494 --> 00:41:44,435
Beautiful thought there.

645
00:41:45,375 --> 00:41:49,577
So Heidegger then says, the principal characteristic of being responsible is this starting

646
00:41:49,677 --> 00:41:51,618
something on its way into arrivals.

647
00:41:53,487 --> 00:41:57,630
On the basis of a look at what the Greeks experienced in being responsible, we now give this

648
00:41:57,670 --> 00:42:00,091
verb to occasion a more inclusive meaning.

649
00:42:00,831 --> 00:42:02,652
So that's what causality is doing.

650
00:42:03,573 --> 00:42:10,037
That's what instrumentality and causality are involving, according to Heidegger.

651
00:42:10,617 --> 00:42:11,238
So he says,

652
00:42:13,559 --> 00:42:14,279
in what then

653
00:42:15,340 --> 00:42:18,602
does the playing in unison of the four ways of occasioning play?

654
00:42:20,143 --> 00:42:21,444
These

655
00:42:22,385 --> 00:42:26,608
let what is not yet presence arrive into presencing.

656
00:42:27,709 --> 00:42:31,412
What is not yet present, what is not yet in being

657
00:42:33,373 --> 00:42:33,874
visible,

658
00:42:34,935 --> 00:42:35,575
tangible,

659
00:42:36,336 --> 00:42:37,356
can now come out.

660
00:42:38,197 --> 00:42:38,798
The silver

661
00:42:39,959 --> 00:42:41,720
can assume the shape of a chalice,

662
00:42:42,460 --> 00:42:43,882
which then can be used

663
00:42:44,742 --> 00:42:46,384
within the context of this ritual.

664
00:42:48,365 --> 00:42:53,563
And the person making it already has all of these things in mind if they really know what they're

665
00:42:53,603 --> 00:42:53,883
doing.

666
00:42:54,803 --> 00:42:55,303
So he says,

667
00:42:56,003 --> 00:43:01,204
accordingly, they're unifiedly governed by a bringing that brings what presence is into appearance.

668
00:43:02,205 --> 00:43:05,465
And he makes this reference to Plato's Symposium at this point.

669
00:43:06,566 --> 00:43:11,247
Every occasion for whatever passes beyond the non-present and goes forward into presencing

670
00:43:11,307 --> 00:43:12,587
is poiesis, bringing forth.

671
00:43:12,607 --> 00:43:14,965
That's Heidegger's gloss on this passage.

672
00:43:15,525 --> 00:43:19,226
Things are coming from non-being or non-presence into presence.

673
00:43:19,666 --> 00:43:22,607
And he says that we're not only talking here about

674
00:43:23,327 --> 00:43:28,368
handicraft manufacture, not only artistic and poetic bringing into appearance and concrete

675
00:43:28,428 --> 00:43:29,928
imagery. So we're not just talking about

676
00:43:30,708 --> 00:43:37,450
the weaver, the shoemaker, the poet, the politician who

677
00:43:37,510 --> 00:43:38,830
brings order into things.

678
00:43:39,864 --> 00:43:45,388
We're also talking about the realm of what the Greeks called phusis, which is often translated

679
00:43:45,469 --> 00:43:50,513
as nature. And Heidegger, whenever he brings up the word phusis, he almost always picks up

680
00:43:50,793 --> 00:43:52,514
on the notion of upspringing.

681
00:43:54,015 --> 00:43:55,256
Things in nature

682
00:43:56,457 --> 00:44:01,121
swell up. They come out of their potentiality into existence.

683
00:44:01,141 --> 00:44:02,882
Think about a seed blossoming.

684
00:44:03,023 --> 00:44:04,564
This is a beautiful thing to watch.

685
00:44:05,384 --> 00:44:06,045
Think about a plant seed.

686
00:44:08,990 --> 00:44:15,975
whether it's a monocot or dicot, it's always a beautiful thing to see, the rupture of the

687
00:44:16,075 --> 00:44:21,579
seed husk, the new plant coming forth and seeking out sustenance.

688
00:44:22,560 --> 00:44:24,021
Maybe it'll die, maybe it won't.

689
00:44:24,061 --> 00:44:27,463
Maybe it'll root itself and grow and become something.

690
00:44:27,764 --> 00:44:31,666
But that's the realm of Fusus, and this takes place within Fusus as well.

691
00:44:31,686 --> 00:44:33,628
So he says...

692
00:44:34,717 --> 00:44:34,977
Now,

693
00:44:35,758 --> 00:44:40,982
what is brought forth by the artisan or the artist has the eruption belonging to bringing forth,

694
00:44:42,082 --> 00:44:44,764
but not in itself the way that nature does,

695
00:44:46,586 --> 00:44:47,306
but in another.

696
00:44:48,327 --> 00:44:49,047
Who's the other?

697
00:44:50,629 --> 00:44:53,991
The other is the silversmith, the craftsman or the artist.

698
00:44:55,720 --> 00:44:58,722
So Heidegger says, So what we have here is a metaphysics in which that which was not comes

699
00:44:58,742 --> 00:45:01,824
to be, and doesn't come to be just immediately, but oftentimes in steps, in stages, in processes.

700
00:45:23,837 --> 00:45:27,158
Sometimes it comes to be by its own internal process of development.

701
00:45:27,818 --> 00:45:32,200
Sometimes it comes to be by something taking it and saying, I'm going to do something better

702
00:45:32,260 --> 00:45:34,941
with you. I'm going to make you do something.

703
00:45:35,561 --> 00:45:37,722
I'm going to take this flint here, which

704
00:45:39,162 --> 00:45:44,464
theoretically could turn itself into an arrowhead, but probably isn't going to, and I'm going

705
00:45:44,504 --> 00:45:47,005
to wrap it until it becomes an arrowhead.

706
00:45:47,025 --> 00:45:49,386
I don't know if this is the right technique for doing that.

707
00:45:49,746 --> 00:45:53,107
I'm just sort of making that up imaginatively, but you get the point.

708
00:45:53,902 --> 00:45:54,522
So he says,

709
00:45:58,605 --> 00:46:02,888
how does this bringing forth happen, whether it's in nature or handicrafted art?

710
00:46:04,128 --> 00:46:06,310
Occasioning has to do with the presencing,

711
00:46:07,610 --> 00:46:12,694
bringing into presence, of that which at any time comes to appearance in the bringing forth.

712
00:46:12,794 --> 00:46:17,076
Bringing forth brings out of, here we have some other important Heideggerian terms,

713
00:46:17,897 --> 00:46:21,279
bringing forth means going from concealment,

714
00:46:24,432 --> 00:46:24,772
to

715
00:46:26,734 --> 00:46:27,454
unconcealment.

716
00:46:35,901 --> 00:46:39,223
The chalice is in a certain respect there in the silver,

717
00:46:41,385 --> 00:46:46,569
but it takes the chalice maker, the silversmith, to make that be the case.

718
00:46:48,070 --> 00:46:48,270
Now,

719
00:46:49,051 --> 00:46:50,252
the Greek word for this is

720
00:46:53,615 --> 00:46:54,455
is Aletheia.

721
00:46:56,916 --> 00:46:58,316
That's also the word for truth.

722
00:47:00,477 --> 00:47:04,458
So Heidegger is playing off of this, this very typical Heidegger, and saying

723
00:47:05,379 --> 00:47:06,059
the truth

724
00:47:06,939 --> 00:47:09,420
of the thing is its unconcealment.

725
00:47:09,560 --> 00:47:10,940
It's coming to being

726
00:47:11,660 --> 00:47:12,261
out of,

727
00:47:13,281 --> 00:47:13,901
literally,

728
00:47:15,522 --> 00:47:18,763
non-being, other being, nothing, whatever you want to say.

729
00:47:19,383 --> 00:47:19,863
So he says,

730
00:47:21,396 --> 00:47:24,979
This coming rests and moves freely within what we call revealing.

731
00:47:25,179 --> 00:47:27,761
The Greeks have this word aletheia for revealing.

732
00:47:28,201 --> 00:47:30,783
The Romans translate this with veritas.

733
00:47:30,823 --> 00:47:34,886
We say truth and usually understand it as correctness of representation.

734
00:47:37,448 --> 00:47:37,649
So

735
00:47:38,930 --> 00:47:40,851
he's going to ask now, where have we strayed to?

736
00:47:40,971 --> 00:47:42,692
How did we end up here

737
00:47:43,453 --> 00:47:45,114
from technology?

738
00:47:46,796 --> 00:47:52,583
And he's going to take stock for a moment and then he's going to move further into this question

739
00:47:53,504 --> 00:47:54,485
of technology,

740
00:47:55,445 --> 00:48:00,328
taking truth and unconcealment as our leading clue.

741
